// C primitive: C builds a reflection list, such as `#cases` or `#members`, by
// constructing the collection its grammar field declares and calling that
// collection's one function applying @insertion once per declaration, in order.
//
// Law, checked in C because macro bodies cannot inspect a function's types
// yet: a type declares at most one @insertion function, and it takes one
// element and adds it. The element is one parameter of the type the type's
// @iteration function yields, when it declares one. C finds the function by
// this role and passes the element under the function's own parameter label;
// it never names the function, its parameter, or the type.
@builtin
macro insertion(): Function
